For larger noise levels, the most-likely fluid case may not be the model with the
smallest "chisquare fit", as explained in other Delivery documentation.
To illustrate this for the problem, let's look at trace 15, which has "gas".
The peak amplitude is approx. 0.15.
If we wind the noise level up to 50% of the peak amplitude (say 0.08)

<noise_rms>0.08</noise_rms>

and run the inversion, you'll see gas is not the maximum MML model for the gas-traces (e.g. cdp=15):

delivery ModelDescription.xml -m prior_traces.su -v 3 --MAP --trace-filter 'cdp=15' -N 0

Oil comes in at about 35%, gas & brine are less.

Again, this comes from the extra probability for oil due to the wider
posterior distribution for the reservoir top time, and the "Occam factor" that goes along with it.

To explore why:
Realisations for oil:

% delivery ModelDescription.xml -m prior_traces.su -v 3 --trace-filter 'cdp=15' --IF 1 -RWS -N 1000 -o realisations.IF1.su

Realisations for Gas:

% delivery ModelDescription.xml -m prior_traces.su -v 3 --trace-filter 'cdp=15' --IF 2 -RWS -N 1000 -o realisations.IF2.su

% cat realisations.IF1.su realisations.IF2.su | deliveryAnalyser --trace-filter 'cdp=15' --stddev t 2

or visually:

% cat realisations.IF1.su realisations.IF2.su | deliveryAnalyser --trace-filter 'cdp=15' --histogram t 2 -G

Nonetheless, full joint realisations throw in a liberal mix of all cases....

% delivery ModelDescription.xml -m prior_traces.su -v 3 --trace-filter 'cdp=15' -RWS -N 1000
deliveryAnalyser -i realisations.su --trace-filter 'cdp=15' -lf -G
